estutils` already has an open issue and I'm limited to proposing just one task, `cmd/ts` makes sense as the next step—I can note the dependency on `pkg/testutils` in the issue even though it's not yet complete. the existing open issue covers pkg/testutils, my 1 task should be for `cmd/ts`.\n\nLet me think about what `cmd/ts` needs:\n- prd004-ts covers all requirements: R1-R9\n- The use case is rel01.0-uc001-ts\n- Files needed: `cmd/ts/main.go`\n\nPer P2 (task sizing): \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" So I should split cmd/ts into implementation and tests. But I'm limited to 1 task. \n\nPer P2: \"If the types and interfaces are small enough to fit within the implementation task's line budget, combine steps 1 and 2 into one task.\" The ts utility doesn't have complex types, so implementation could be one task. But tests are always separate.\n\nGiven the 1-task constraint, I should propose the implementation of `cmd/ts/main.go` (implementation only, not tests), since tests would be a separate task.\n\nLooking at prd004-ts, I need to cover the full scope: default behavior with various output formats, incremental mode, elapsed-since-start mode, monotonic clock mode, relative-time conversion mode, proper exit codes and error handling, and environment variable support.\n\nPer P9 (requirement granularity): \"Code tasks target 5-8 requirements\". The ts utility has 9 requirement groups (R1-R9), with R9 being differential testing. I could tackle R1-R8 in the implementation task and leave R9 for a test task.\n\nHowever, I'm limited to 1 task, and P2 says implementation and tests must be separate. Since I can only propose one task, I'll focus on the core implementation (R1-R8) for the `cmd/ts` utility, which reads stdin line by line and prepends strftime-formatted timestamps with support for various flags like -i.\n\nThe implementation will live in `cmd/ts/main.go` and doesn't require any pkg/ dependencies. I'm estimating around 200-300 lines given the complexity of handling multiple timestamp formats and flag combinations. Per P9, this covers 5-8 requirements at the group level, and I'll anchor it to prd004-ts R1-R8 as specified in P6.\n\nFor the task definition, the stitch agent will need the primary spec from prd004-ts.yaml, the use case context, project architecture, and module information. The main file to create is `cmd/ts/main.go`, and I'm breaking down the requirements into four groups: default behavior without flags, custom format strings with subsecond extensions, incremental mode, and elapsed-since-start mode.\n\nI also need to implement monotonic clock mode, relative-time conversion mode, proper exit codes and error handling, and respect the TZ environment variable. For the design approach, the tool reads only from stdin, leverages Go's built-in monotonic clock readings, and I'm building a custom strftime converter to handle the format specifications.\n\nThe converter maps standard % codes to Go's time.Format equivalents, with special handling for ts-specific subsecond extensions like %.S, %.s, and %.T. For elapsed-time modes, I'll internally set TZ=GMT to ensure consistent formatting. The -i and -s flags are mutually exclusive with each other and with -r. For acceptance testing, I need to verify that basic timestamp output matches the expected pattern, that elapsed-time mode produces monotonically increasing values, and that additional criteria are met. I need to pull in the concrete design choices from the PRD rather than making assumptions. The sub-nanosecond precision requirement with six decimal places is explicitly stated, as is the monotonic clock requirement. The TZ environment variable constraint for GMT is also specified in the requirements, so I should include these as derived design decisions rather than invented ones.\n\nFor the file structure, since `cmd/ts` is a main package without exported types, `main.go` is the standard convention and aligns with the architecture guidance.\n\nNow I'm drafting the task definition with all the key requirements from the PRD—the stdin timestamping behavior, flag modes, strftime format handling, and timezone rules—along with the necessary documentation references and file structure. Implement default behavior per prd004-ts R1.1-R1.6: read stdin line by line, prepend timestamp in format '%b %d %H:%M:%S', flush stdout after each line, preserve newlines, pass through partial lines, exit 0 on EOF. Support custom strftime format strings with ts-specific subsecond extensions like %.S for seconds.microseconds and %.s for unix_epoch.microseconds, leveraging Go's nanosecond precision. Add incremental mode (-i) to show elapsed time since the previous line, elapsed-since-start mode (-s) for monotonically increasing timestamps, monotonic clock mode (-m) using Go's time.Now(), and relative-time conversion mode (-r) to scan input lines for syslog, ISO-8601, and RFC 2822 timestamps.\n\nImplement exit codes and error handling: exit 0 on clean EOF, exit non-zero with usage to stderr on unrecognized or mutually exclusive flags. Respect the TZ environment variable for wall-clock timestamps, but force TZ=GMT internally in elapsed-time modes. The design leverages Go's built-in monotonic clock support and nanosecond precision to handle all timing requirements efficiently. I need to use bufio.Scanner to read newline-delimited input from stdin. I also need to enforce mutual exclusivity between flags: -i and -s can't be used together, and -r can't be combined with either of them, with usage errors printed to stderr on violation. The acceptance criteria show that the default timestamp format should be '%b %d %H:%M:%S', and elapsed time in -s mode needs to be monotonically non-decreasing. The build command succeeds and produces the binary, and the code passes linting checks without warnings. Let me verify the counts are within range—8 requirements, 8 acceptance criteria, and 5 design decisions all fit the target parameters for a code task.\n\nSince this task focuses on `cmd/ts/main.go` rather than the existing `pkg/testutils` issue, there's no dependency within this batch, so I'll set it to -1.\n\nI'm also verifying that I'm not duplicating work - the existing open issue covers the differential testing harness for `pkg/testutils`, while my proposed task is specifically for the `cmd/ts` implementation. These are separate packages and PRDs, so no overlap. I'm intentionally excluding R9 (differential testing) from this implementation task since that's testing work that belongs in a separate task, which aligns with keeping implementation and tests split.\n\nThe scope looks reasonable - implementing the ts logic in `cmd/ts/main.go` should be roughly 200-300 lines based on the Perl source, which fits the task size expectations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73877,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73877},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccbc136f-830d-4c55-a241-1238fc961e39","uuid":"618e0890-bbdc-4d28-b7a1-398da1185fb7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01G1BVHNLKayrsbUESFrkzvB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nAll project information is inline in the prompt. No tool calls needed.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications, causing re-implementations to diverge in edge cases. This project derives formal specs from C source, generates Go implementations via Claude agents, and verifies parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `docs/` — Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n- `cmd/<utility>/` — Go implementations (one `main.go` + `<utility>_test.go` per utility)\n- `pkg/testutils/` — Differential testing harness (prd001)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Syscall abstractions (prd002)\n- `pkg/format/` — Output formatting (prd003)\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build targets\n\n**Current state:** Working branch is spec-only. Generated code from previous runs lives at tagged commits, not on this branch. All use cases show `codestatus: not started`.\n\n**Current release:** rel01.0 — single use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (status: in_progress).\n\n## Step 3: Reason About Priorities\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.0** with use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (cmd/ts implementation).\n\nExisting open issue `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-dtl` already covers `pkg/testutils` (prd001 R1-R5) — must not duplicate.\n\nThe next untracked work is `cmd/ts/main.go` (prd004-ts R1-R8). Tests are a separate task per P2. Per P5, this depends on pkg/testutils being built first (the existing open issue), but the implementation itself can be authored independently — it doesn't import pkg/testutils; only the test file will.\n\n1-task limit: propose `cmd/ts` implementation (prd004-ts R1-R8, excluding R9 differential tests which are a separate task).\n\n## Step 4: Proposed Task\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/ts timestamp stdin utility (prd004-ts R1-R8)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml (all requirements R1-R9 and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.0-uc001-ts.yaml (use case flow and success criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ package conventions -- one main.go per utility, binary built to bin/)\n      - go.mod (module name: github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Complete ts implementation: stdin timestamping, all flag modes (-i -s -m -r), custom strftime format support including subsecond extensions\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default behavior per prd004-ts R1.1-R1.6: read stdin line by line with bufio.Scanner, prepend timestamp in format '%b %d %H:%M:%S', flush stdout after each line, preserve original newlines, pass through partial lines (no trailing newline), exit 0 on EOF, do not exit non-zero on SIGPIPE\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Support custom strftime format string as optional positional argument per prd004-ts R2.1-R2.4: implement a converter mapping POSIX strftime % codes to Go time.Format layout strings; handle ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S (seconds.microseconds), %.s (unix_epoch.microseconds), %.T (HH:MM:SS.microseconds) by extracting UnixNano and formatting to six decimal places; obtain both second and microsecond components from a single time.Now() call per line\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement incremental mode (-i) per prd004-ts R3.1-R3.4: timestamp shows elapsed time since the previous line (first line: elapsed since start), default format '%H:%M:%S' with TZ=GMT; -i and -s are mutually exclusive, passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement elapsed-since-start mode (-s) per prd004-ts R4.1-R4.3: timestamp shows monotonically increasing elapsed time since ts started, default format '%H:%M:%S' with TZ=GMT; a custom format argument overrides the default\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement monotonic clock mode (-m) per prd004-ts R5.1-R5.3: use Go's time.Now() which automatically includes a monotonic reading; -m is compatible with all format modes and custom format strings including subsecond extensions; timestamps must not jump backwards\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement relative-time conversion mode (-r) per prd004-ts R6.1-R6.5: scan each input line for syslog ('Jan  5 14:30:00'), ISO-8601 ('2024-01-05T14:30:00.000Z'), RFC 2822 ('16 Jun 94 07:29:35 GMT'), and lastlog ('Mon Jan  5 14:30') timestamp patterns; replace matches with human-readable relative age (e.g. '15m5s ago'); when -r and a format string are both given, convert matched timestamps to the specified strftime format instead; pass through lines with no recognizable timestamp unchanged; -r is mutually exclusive with -i and -s\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement exit codes and error handling per prd004-ts R7.1-R7.3: exit 0 on clean EOF; exit non-zero and print usage message to stderr on unrecognized flag or mutually exclusive flag combination (-i with -s, -r with -i, -r with -s)\"\n      - id: R8\n        text: \"Respect TZ environment variable per prd004-ts R8.1-R8.2: use user's TZ for wall-clock timestamp formatting in default and -m modes; force TZ=GMT internally in -i and -s modes for elapsed-time formatting regardless of the user's TZ setting, matching the Perl source behavior\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Go's time.Now() automatically includes a monotonic reading (prd004-ts R5.1). Use time.Now() for both wall-clock and monotonic timestamp sampling. Use time.Since() for elapsed calculations in -i and -s modes. Do not call CLOCK_MONOTONIC via syscall.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"The subsecond extensions %.S, %.s, %.T are ts-specific and have no Go time.Format equivalent (prd004-ts R2.3). Implement a custom strftime-to-Go-format converter that pre-processes the format string, extracts these extensions, calls time.Now() once per line, and splices in the microsecond-formatted values.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"In -i and -s modes, set the TZ environment variable to GMT at startup before any time formatting occurs, matching the Perl source behavior (prd004-ts R8.2, R3.2, R4.2). Use time.LoadLocation and time.In to apply the zone without mutating the global environment when possible; fall back to os.Setenv if necessary.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"cmd/ts reads from stdin only, never from files (prd004-ts non-goals). Use bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin) with default line-splitting. Do not implement --help or --version beyond a usage string printed to stderr on bad input (prd004-ts non-goals).\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Flag parsing uses the standard flag package. Mutually exclusive flag pairs (-i/-s, -r/-i, -r/-s) are checked after flag.Parse() returns; violations print a usage message to stderr via fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...) and call os.Exit with a non-zero status (prd004-ts R3.4, R6.5, R7.2).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"'echo hello | ./bin/ts' produces exactly one line matching the pattern '<3-letter-month> <1-2-digit-day> <HH:MM:SS> hello' where the timestamp uses the default format '%b %d %H:%M:%S' (prd004-ts AC1)\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"'printf a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n | ./bin/ts -s' produces three lines each prefixed with an HH:MM:SS elapsed timestamp; the elapsed values are non-negative and non-decreasing across lines (prd004-ts AC2)\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"'printf first\\\\nsecond\\\\nthird\\\\n | ./bin/ts -i' produces three lines each prefixed with an HH:MM:SS per-line incremental timestamp\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Empty stdin (immediate EOF via 'true | ./bin/ts') produces no output and exits 0 (prd004-ts AC5)\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"'ts -i -s' prints a message containing 'usage' (case-insensitive) to stderr and exits with a non-zero status (prd004-ts R3.4)\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"'ts -z' (unrecognized flag) prints a usage message to stderr and exits non-zero (prd004-ts R7.2)\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"'go build -o bin/ts ./cmd/ts' succeeds with no compilation errors and produces the binary at bin/ts\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"cmd/ts source compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint when run against cmd/ts/... (prd004-ts AC6)\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73877,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73877},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccbc136f-830d-4c55-a241-1238fc961e39","uuid":"f0c920c2-4949-46b3-8980-222f3f281647"}
